

import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

# 1. Load Dataset
df = pd.read_csv("City_day.csv")

# 2. Display Dataset Information
print("First 5 Records:")
print(df.head())

print("\nDataset Shape:")
print(df.shape)

print("\nColumn Names:")
print(df.columns.tolist())

print("\nDataset Information:")
df.info()

print("\nMissing Values:")
print(df.isnull().sum())

# 3. Convert Date Column
df["Date"] = pd.to_datetime(
    df["Date"],
    format="%Y-%m-%d"
)

# 4. Summary Statistics
print("\nSummary Statistics:")
print(df.describe())

# 5. AQI Trend Line Graph
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
plt.plot(df["Date"], df["AQI"], marker="o")
plt.title("AQI Trend Over Time")
plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("AQI")
plt.xticks(rotation=45)
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# 6. Pollutant Levels Line Graph
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
plt.plot(df["Date"], df["PM2.5"], label="PM2.5")
plt.plot(df["Date"], df["PM10"], label="PM10")
plt.plot(df["Date"], df["CO"], label="CO")
plt.title("Pollutant Levels Over Time")
plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("Pollutant Level")
plt.legend()
plt.xticks(rotation=45)
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# 7. Bar Graph
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))
plt.bar(df["Date"], df["AQI"])
plt.title("AQI Comparison by Date")
plt.xlabel("Date")
plt.ylabel("AQI")
plt.xticks(rotation=45)
plt.show()

# 8. Box Plot
plt.figure(figsize=(7, 5))
plt.boxplot(df["AQI"].dropna())
plt.title("AQI Distribution")
plt.ylabel("AQI")
plt.show()

# 9. Scatter Plot: PM2.5 vs AQI
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.scatter(df["PM2.5"], df["AQI"])
plt.title("PM2.5 vs AQI")
plt.xlabel("PM2.5")
plt.ylabel("AQI")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# 10. Scatter Plot: PM10 vs AQI
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.scatter(df["PM10"], df["AQI"])
plt.title("PM10 vs AQI")
plt.xlabel("PM10")
plt.ylabel("AQI")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# 11. Scatter Plot: CO vs AQI
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.scatter(df["CO"], df["AQI"])
plt.title("CO vs AQI")
plt.xlabel("CO")
plt.ylabel("AQI")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()



This practical uses Pandas to load and analyze the dataset and Matplotlib to create different graphs.
1. Import libraries
import pandas as pdimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt



- import pandas as pd – Imports Pandas for reading and analyzing data.
- import matplotlib.pyplot as plt – Imports Matplotlib for creating graphs.
- pd and plt are short names (aliases) used to write code easily.
2. Load dataset
df = pd.read_csv("City_day.csv")



- pd.read_csv() – Reads a CSV file.
- "City_day.csv" – Name of the dataset file.
- df – DataFrame that stores the dataset.
3. Display dataset information
print("First 5 Records:")print(df.head())



- print() – Displays output.
- df.head() – Displays the first 5 rows of the dataset.
- It helps us understand the structure and values of the data.
print("\nDataset Shape:")print(df.shape)



- \n – Prints a new line.
- df.shape – Displays the number of rows and columns.
- Example: (1000, 16) means 1000 rows and 16 columns.
print("\nColumn Names:")print(df.columns.tolist())



- df.columns – Displays all column names.
- .tolist() – Converts column names into a Python list.
print("\nDataset Information:")df.info()



- df.info() – Displays dataset information.
- It shows column names, data types and non-null values.
- It helps us identify missing values and data types.
print("\nMissing Values:")print(df.isnull().sum())



- df.isnull() – Checks for missing values.
- .sum() – Counts missing values in each column.
- It helps us identify incomplete data.
4. Convert Date column
df["Date"] = pd.to_datetime(    df["Date"],    format="%Y-%m-%d")



- df["Date"] – Selects the Date column.
- pd.to_datetime() – Converts date values into datetime format.
- format="%Y-%m-%d" – Specifies the date format:
  - %Y – Year
  - %m – Month
  - %d – Day
Why do we convert the date?

To make date-based analysis and plotting easier.
5. Summary statistics
print("\nSummary Statistics:")print(df.describe())



- df.describe() – Displays statistical information about numerical columns.
- It gives:
  - count – Number of non-missing values.
  - mean – Average value.
  - std – Standard deviation.
  - min – Minimum value.
  - 25% – First quartile.
  - 50% – Median.
  - 75% – Third quartile.
  - max – Maximum value.
6. AQI Trend Line Graph
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))plt.plot(df["Date"], df["AQI"], marker="o")plt.title("AQI Trend Over Time")plt.xlabel("Date")plt.ylabel("AQI")plt.xticks(rotation=45)plt.grid(True)plt.show()



Line-by-line explanation:
- plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5)) – Creates a graph of width 10 and height 5.
- plt.plot() – Creates a line graph.
- df["Date"] – X-axis values.
- df["AQI"] – Y-axis values.
- marker="o" – Displays circular markers on data points.
- plt.title() – Adds the graph title.
- plt.xlabel() – Gives the X-axis name.
- plt.ylabel() – Gives the Y-axis name.
- plt.xticks(rotation=45) – Rotates date labels by 45 degrees.
- plt.grid(True) – Displays grid lines.
- plt.show() – Displays the graph.
Purpose: To understand how Air Quality Index changes over time.
7. Pollutant Levels Line Graph
plt.figure(figsize=(10, 5))plt.plot(df["Date"], df["PM2.5"], label="PM2.5")plt.plot(df["Date"], df["PM10"], label="PM10")plt.plot(df["Date"], df["CO"], label="CO")plt.title("Pollutant Levels Over Time")plt.xlabel("Date")plt.ylabel("Pollutant Level")plt.legend()plt.xticks(rotation=45)plt.grid(True)plt.show()



Explanation:
